# XSH fgetc()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.

assertion  XSH.fgetc.001
anchor     functions/fgetc.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fgetc.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       On a stream reading a file that holds the bytes 0x41, 0xff,
           0x00, 0x42, successive fgetc() calls return 0x41, 0xff, 0 and
           0x42 (each byte as an unsigned char value, so 0xff is not
           EOF), and ftell() after each call is 1, 2, 3 and 4.
test       t/001-bytes.c

assertion  XSH.fgetc.002
anchor     functions/fgetc.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       On a stream at the end of its file (an empty file, and a file
           "ab" after two reads), fgetc() returns EOF, feof() is then true
           and ferror() false.
test       t/002-eof.c

assertion  XSH.fgetc.003
anchor     functions/fgetc.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     functions/fgetc.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       On a stream reading "ab" whose end-of-file indicator is set,
           after "c" is appended to the file through a separate open(),
           fgetc() returns EOF and feof() stays true; after clearerr()
           the next fgetc() returns 'c'.
note       Issue 6 (C99 alignment) made the end-of-file indicator decide
           the result. The appended byte comes through another open file
           description, so the handle rules of XSH 2.5.1 do not apply.
test       t/003-eof-sticky.c

assertion  XSH.fgetc.004
anchor     functions/fgetc.html#ERRORS/EBADF
anchor     functions/fgetc.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       On a stream opened "r" whose descriptor was closed before the
           first read, fgetc() returns EOF, sets errno to EBADF and sets
           the stream's error indicator.
test       t/004-ebadf-closed.c

assertion  XSH.fgetc.005
anchor     functions/fgetc.html#ERRORS/EBADF
anchor     functions/fgetc.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       On a stream opened "w" (its descriptor open for writing only),
           fgetc() returns EOF, sets errno to EBADF and sets the stream's
           error indicator.
test       t/005-ebadf-write-only.c

assertion  XSH.fgetc.006
anchor     functions/fgetc.html#ERRORS/EAGAIN
anchor     functions/fgetc.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       On a stream on the read end of an empty pipe with O_NONBLOCK
           set (the write end still open), fgetc() returns EOF, sets
           errno to EAGAIN and sets the stream's error indicator.
test       t/006-eagain.c

assertion  XSH.fgetc.007
anchor     functions/fgetc.html#ERRORS/EINTR
anchor     functions/fgetc.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       On a stream on the read end of an empty pipe (blocking), a
           SIGALRM caught without SA_RESTART 1 s into fgetc() makes it
           return EOF with errno EINTR and the error indicator set (a byte
           is written to the pipe from the second alarm on, so an
           implementation that restarts the read returns it and is
           reported).
timeout    20
test       t/007-eintr.c

assertion  XSH.fgetc.008
anchor     functions/fgetc.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       For a file whose last data access time is set to 2001-09-09,
           the first fgetc() on a new stream reading it returns its first
           byte, and fstat() afterwards shows a later st_atim.
note       XBD 4.12: a marked timestamp is updated before fstat()
           returns. A file system mounted noatime fails this, rightly; a
           relatime policy updates it, since the access time is older
           than the modification time.
test       t/008-atime.c
